BT 1 0 0 1 0 85.8 Tm 10.4 TL /F2 7.8 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Answer) Tj /F1 7.8 Tf  T* .862983 Tw (When validation fails the program must ) Tj /F2 7.8 Tf (reject) Tj /F1 7.8 Tf ( the input immediately and must not continue with any partial or default values that could lead to) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.276964 Tw (incorrect behaviour. The preferred response is: \(a\) record a structured log entry containing the field that failed, the received value \(or a safe) Tj T* 0 Tw .075129 Tw (truncated of it\), the expected rule, and a correlation / request ID; \(b\) return a well-defined error to the caller \227 for APIs this is typically HTTP 400 or) Tj T* 0 Tw .491286 Tw (422 together with a machine-readable body that lists each violation; \(c\) never expose internal stack traces or sensitive configuration to the client.) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.33152 Tw (For batch jobs the invalid record can be written to a dead-letter queue or error file while the rest of the batch continues. Retrying the same) Tj T* 0 Tw .647267 Tw (malformed payload is usually pointless; retries should be reserved for transient infrastructure errors, not validation failures. By treating validation) Tj T* 0 Tw .9337 Tw (errors as expected control-flow rather than unexpected exceptions, the application stays stable under both user mistakes and deliberate attack) Tj T* 0 Tw (traffic.) Tj T* ET
